  collapse to a single outcome about the auto-rename contract.
misalignment:
  - spec-bloat
---

## Issues

- [ ] `description` is 176 chars (under cap, fine).

- [ ] `intent` is 77 words (under cap, fine).

- [ ] `outcomes.desired` has 9 items (cap 4). Items 0-5 enumerate one branch
  of `type` per outcome (intent-no-research, intent-with-research, research,
  both, todo, plan). Item 7 is the report-actions-as-prose contract (more
  Strategy than outcome). Item 8 is the MCP-tool-description-drift
  meta-outcome enumerating the 5 `type` values and the auto-rename rule
  verbatim. Compress to 3-4 domain criteria.
  Traces to: `outcomes.desired` | Brevity Contract caps + Forbidden content (CLI flag enumerations, function names)
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity, Forbidden-content

- [ ] `outcomes.undesired` has 5 paragraph-length items including the MCP
  description drift meta-rule. Compress.
  Traces to: `outcomes.undesired` | Brevity Contract caps + Forbidden content
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity

- [ ] `## Good examples` reproduces exact prose-style action reports verbatim
  with exact filename strings (`Created .aide`, `Renamed .aide → intent.aide`,
  `Created research.aide`). Forbidden content (exact strings).
  Traces to: `## Good examples` | Forbidden content
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Forbidden-content

- [ ] `## Bad examples` reproduces a verbatim JSON envelope showing the
  forbidden shape. Forbidden content (JSON).
  Traces to: `## Bad examples` | Forbidden content
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Forbidden-content

## Retro

aide_scaffold's bloat is in the per-`type` enumeration: 6 outcomes each
restating one branch is the textbook anti-pattern of treating each switch
arm as a separate domain criterion. The contract is one outcome: "the
auto-rename rule (`research.aide` introduction renames any existing `.aide`
to `intent.aide`) is enforced; existing files are skipped, never overwritten."
The five `type` values and their per-arm behavior are unit-testable and
documented in code.
